Men who provably banked NO service year (joined the roster after the line):
  total with a next-spring cell      2193
  cell is a bare integer             18   of these, WRONG (reads >= 1 year): 11  61.1%
  cell carries a day count           2175   of these, WRONG (reads >= 1 year): 12  0.6%

So a day-count cell is right 99.4% of the time on this test, and a bare
integer is wrong 61.1% of the time. The two are different populations.

Men who COULD have banked a year (joined on or before the line):
  total with a next-spring cell      187
  cell is a bare integer             65  reading exactly "1": 65

THE BIND. A man who banks exactly one year is written "1". A man whose
service was rounded to a season count is also written "1". The cell cannot
tell them apart, so `mls` can neither confirm the line nor be used to
measure who beat it. Excluding the bare integers removes the confirmations
along with the errors.

